@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title Temporal-Difference Learning — Predict from the Next Prediction
' Paper: Learning to predict by the methods of temporal differences
' URL:   https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00115009
' Level: 1

component "State s_t\nobservation at time t" as S <<app>>
database  "Value function V\nparameters, updated online" as VF <<db>>
component "Observed step\nreward r_t, next state s_t+1" as STEP <<async>>
component "TD target\nr_t + γ·V(s_t+1)" as TGT <<app>>
component "TD error\ntarget − V(s_t)" as ERR <<error>>

S ARROW_MAIN STEP : act, observe
S ARROW_MAIN VF : predict V(s_t)
STEP ARROW_MAIN TGT : reward r_t
STEP ARROW_MAIN VF : next state s_t+1
VF ARROW_MAIN TGT : bootstrap — V(s_t+1) comes\nfrom the function being learned
TGT ARROW_MAIN ERR
VF ARROW_MAIN ERR : current estimate V(s_t)
ERR ARROW_MAIN VF : adjust V(s_t)\ntoward the target

note bottom of ERR
  **The move is to stop waiting for the outcome.**
  A supervised method would update against the final
  result — win or loss, many moves later. TD updates
  against its //own next prediction// plus the observed
  reward, so credit flows backwards one step per
  experience and learning happens **during** the
  episode. The final outcome is therefore absent from
  this diagram on purpose: TD never consumes it
  directly.
  ----
  That is also the catch: the target contains the
  estimate being learned (note ""V"" feeds both the
  target and the error), so TD is a bootstrapping
  method whose stability depends on the step size
  (""../optimization/gradient-descent.puml"") and on γ,
  which sets how far credit reaches.
end note
@enduml
